  "reason": "The records collectively make the later strategy plausibly reconstructible. R1 directly identifies approximately 600 °C annealing as a strong decomposition and hardening treatment in closely related Al-Co-(Cr)-Fe-Ni alloys. R7 shows that gamma-prime/L12-type phase content in a similar Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni-based alloy can decrease upon higher-temperature aging, supporting a non-monotonic ordering response. R8 supplies the complementary interface mechanism in a closely related FCC/B2 eutectic alloy by connecting phase-boundary transition structures with strain localization and cracking. R2, R4, and R19 further connect ordering, modulus changes, plastic instability, and cross-slip constraints. Although none of the records states the exact 600 °C-to-750 °C ductile-brittle-ductile sequence or the specific combination of nearly stoichiometric L12 domains and jagged boundary tips, an expert could plausibly combine the established aging, ordering, and interface-localization observations to propose that intervention and mechanism."
